---
/* The site's first drawing: the loop that is an agent, inside the ring that is
   the orchestrator.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Drawn in HTML rather than shipped as an image, and the choice is the whole
   design: the Face is a terminal UI — panes, hairlines, labels — so boxes made
   of `--rule` borders with eyebrow labels ARE the tool's own face, they follow
   both schemes for free because every colour is a role, and a screen reader
   gets real text instead of an alt attribute doing its best. No gradients, no
   shadows, no arrowheads drawn on a canvas: the arrows are the characters a
   terminal would use.

   One accent, where the thesis is: the return edge. The site's first concept
   says the engineering is not in the model, it is in the loop around it — so
   the loop's closing edge is the one thing here allowed to be `--current`.

   The box labels are set in the mono eyebrow register the site already uses
   for door eyebrows and link kinds — a label, not a claim of machine truth. */
---

<figure class="fig-loop">
  <div class="ring">
    <p class="ring-label">orchestrator · conductor — plan · limits · gates · record</p>

    <div class="loop" role="img" aria-label="The agent loop: context goes to the model, the model acts, the action has a result, and the result becomes the next context. One stretch of this loop under a token ceiling is a session.">
      <div class="row" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="box">context</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">model</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">action</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">result</span>
      </div>
      <p class="return" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="glyph">⟲</span> the result becomes the next context — the loop is the agent
      </p>
      <p class="session" aria-hidden="true">
        one stretch of this loop, under a token ceiling, is a session
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    The agent is the small loop. The orchestrator is the ring around it: it
    starts sessions, stops them at their limits, runs the gates, and writes
    down what happened.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig-loop {
    margin: 0;
    inline-size: min(100%, var(--measure-wide));
  }

  /* The ring is a pane, in the strip's own manner: mantle behind it, a
     hairline around it, an eyebrow naming it. */
  .ring {
    background: var(--mantle);
    border: var(--rule);
    padding: var(--space-m) var(--space-m) var(--space-s);
  }

  .ring-label,
  .box {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .ring-label {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-m);
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .box {
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding-block: var(--space-2xs);
    padding-inline: var(--space-xs);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .arrow {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--mono);
  }

  /* The thesis edge. The one accent in the drawing. */
  .return {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--current);
  }

  .glyph {
    font-family: var(--mono);
  }

  .session {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
